H('Abstract')
P('DocuAudit is a document-processing prototype that extracts structured information from native-text invoice PDFs and records evidence for each field that it accepts. The project addresses a central limitation of automated document extraction: a plausible value may be present in a model response without being the correct value for the requested field. To reduce this risk, extraction and verification are implemented as separate stages. The fixed invoice schema contains invoice number, invoice date, vendor name, due date, subtotal, tax, and total. The intended outcome is not only a set of extracted values, but also a field-level decision that can distinguish supported information from uncertain or absent information.')
P('The system uses PyMuPDF to read PDF text, a RoBERTa SQuAD2 question-answering model to propose field values, BAAI bge-small-en-v1.5 embeddings with Qdrant for document-scoped retrieval, deterministic type and label checks, and an optional DeBERTa natural-language-inference check. A LangGraph workflow coordinates retrieval, extraction, compliance screening, audit, one bounded repair pass, and final routing. FastAPI accepts uploads and serves document results; Celery and Redis move long-running processing out of the request path; PostgreSQL stores workflow and audit records; and a Streamlit interface supports upload, status polling, result inspection, and a basic review queue. Compliance checks include selected person/location entity detection, duplicate invoice-number lookup, subtotal-plus-tax arithmetic, and a configured amount threshold.')
P('The repository reports evaluation on 27 synthetic invoices containing 186 labelled field values. On the final development benchmark, the default veto configuration reports field F1, verified precision, coverage, compliance flag accuracy, and absent-field handling of 1.000, with three of 27 invoices routed to review because they had no due date. In an earlier run on ten then-held-out invoices, the reported field F1 was 0.986 and verified precision was 1.000; those examples were later incorporated into the development set after the results informed fixes. The NLI ablation reported that strict mode increased review cases without improving verified precision on this benchmark, while veto and rules-only modes had the same measured outcomes.')
P('These scores demonstrate behavior on a small, constructed dataset and do not establish accuracy on real business invoices. The project is limited to native-text PDFs, uses simplified amount and label rules, and does not provide OCR, page-coordinate provenance, or a reviewer correction and approval workflow. The work concludes that evidence-based audit and explicit review routing are useful design patterns, while larger independent evaluation, stronger provenance, data protection controls, and human correction are required before operational use.')
P('Keywords: invoice processing, information extraction, evidence verification, natural language inference, document AI, human review')
