H('1. Introduction')
P('DocuAudit is a software project that processes native-text invoice PDFs and produces a structured set of invoice fields together with checks that explain whether each field is supported by the document. The prototype combines document ingestion, semantic retrieval, extractive question answering, deterministic validation, optional natural-language inference, compliance screening, persistence, and a web interface. Its central design choice is to separate proposing a value from deciding whether the value should be treated as verified. If the checks cannot support a value, the system keeps that uncertainty visible and routes the document to review.')
P('This introduction sets out why invoice processing is a suitable but challenging automation problem, defines the project problem and objectives, explains the contribution and research questions, and states the intended outcomes. The implementation focuses on seven common invoice fields and on evaluating correctness and safe refusal on a small synthetic dataset. The project is therefore a measured engineering prototype rather than a general document-understanding product or a replacement for financial approval controls.')
H('1.1 Context',2); P('Invoices contain recurring business fields, but wording, formatting, placement, and layout vary. Automation can reduce data entry; however, silently accepting a wrong amount, date, or supplier reference can create downstream accounting and compliance problems. Extraction quality alone does not establish that a value is supported by its source.')
P('A single invoice may display several dates, identifiers, and totals. “Invoice Date” can appear near “Due Date”; a purchase-order reference may look like an invoice number; subtotal, tax, and amount due may all be numeric and close together. Different suppliers place labels beside values, above them, or in separate columns. A document system must therefore interpret both the text of a value and its local context. A span that looks like a valid date is not sufficient evidence that it is the payment date requested by the application.')
P('Document automation also creates a distinction between throughput and trust. A pipeline can return a complete set of values quickly while still making an undetected error. In a financial setting, an incorrect accepted amount can affect payment, reconciliation, compliance review, or supplier communication. Conversely, a system that rejects every uncertain value may protect against false acceptance but provide little automation. The project treats these outcomes as separate measures and uses review routing as a controlled response to uncertainty.')
P('DocuAudit narrows the input domain to PDFs with machine-readable text. This restriction makes the initial system easier to evaluate because it can concentrate on field extraction and evidence checking rather than also solving optical character recognition and page-layout reconstruction. It remains an important limitation: a scanned invoice or complex line-item table may not produce a usable text stream and is outside the tested scope.')
H('1.2 Problem Statement',2); P('This project addresses the risk that a language-model document pipeline returns plausible values without a reliable link to the source. The engineering task is to extract a fixed invoice schema, check each candidate against the text and its label, retain supporting evidence, and route unsupported fields for review.')
P('The core design problem has two separate questions for every field: did the extractor find a plausible value, and does the invoice text justify treating that value as correct for this field? These questions cannot be collapsed into one model confidence score. A number may appear on the page and still be the subtotal rather than the total, or an invoice date rather than a payment due date. The system therefore records extraction output and audit decisions separately, allowing an operator to see when a value was proposed but not verified.')
P('The project addresses the gap between an extracted answer and a trustworthy field value. Model output may be truncated, may select a nearby label rather than its value, or may answer a related but different field question. If the system returns only the value, downstream users cannot easily tell whether it was found in the PDF, inferred from surrounding context, or confused with another amount or date. The engineering problem is to make that decision inspectable and to avoid treating unsupported values as successful results.')
P('The project also addresses a workflow problem. Transformer inference may take longer than an ordinary web request, so document processing should continue independently of the browser connection. The system must persist enough state to let a client query progress and later inspect the result. It must distinguish a processing exception from an invoice that was processed successfully but still needs a human to resolve one or more fields.')
P('The scope is intentionally bounded. DocuAudit does not attempt to validate whether an invoice is legally valid, whether a supplier is genuine, or whether payment should be authorized. Its configured compliance checks are limited to selected entities and simple rules. The primary problem is field-level extraction and evidence verification under a declared input and schema, with a clear route for uncertainty.')
H('1.3 Aim and Objectives',2); P('The aim is to design, implement, and evaluate a deployable prototype for invoice processing that prioritizes traceability and safe refusal alongside extraction quality. “Deployable” here means that the repository provides an integrated API, worker, storage services, and interface that can be run using its documented development setup; it does not imply production readiness or a service-level guarantee.')
P('The objectives translate this aim into implementation and evaluation tasks:')
for x in ['Extract invoice number, invoice date, vendor name, due date, subtotal, tax, and total from native-text PDF invoices.','Associate candidates with source evidence and validate field type and label.','Check arithmetic consistency, a configured amount threshold, duplicate invoice numbers per vendor, and selected person/location entities.','Attempt one bounded repair pass and accept a replacement only after verification.','Expose asynchronous processing through an API and review interface.','Measure extraction, verified precision, verification coverage, compliance flag accuracy, and missing-field handling.']: B(x)
P('The objectives are evaluated at different levels. Field extraction is measured against annotated values; audit behavior is measured by whether accepted values are correct and absent fields remain unsupported; compliance behavior is measured by exact flag agreement; and the software architecture is assessed by whether a document can move from upload through asynchronous processing to a queryable result. This separation prevents one aggregate score from concealing weaknesses in a particular stage.')
H('1.4 Contribution',2); P('The main contribution is a pattern in which extraction proposes values, an independent audit checks them against labelled evidence, and unresolved fields remain visible for human review. A regression gate treats any wrong verified value as a failure.')
P('The implementation contribution is an end-to-end demonstration of that pattern across asynchronous ingestion, retrieval, extraction, compliance checks, bounded repair, persistence, and a review-oriented interface. The evaluation contribution is an explicit comparison of three NLI policies and metrics that distinguish extraction similarity, verification coverage, and correctness among accepted values. The prototype is deliberately scoped so these behaviors can be measured on a small, labelled invoice set.')
P('A further contribution is the explicit representation of uncertainty as a normal workflow outcome. The system does not require every field to be populated before a document can finish processing. Instead, it can retain a candidate, attach an unsupported decision, and route the invoice to needs_review. This is important for missing values: an absent due date should remain absent rather than being guessed to satisfy the schema.')
P('The report also documents the boundary of the evidence. It distinguishes final development-set results from an earlier held-out measurement whose examples were later used during tuning. It discusses storage, data privacy, dependency management, and operational gaps so that the prototype’s academic results are not mistaken for a guarantee of production performance.')
H('1.5 Research Questions',2)
for x in ['Can a fixed-schema question-answering pipeline extract common invoice values from native-text PDFs? This question examines whether extractive QA can populate a practical invoice schema when input text is machine-readable and the requested fields are known in advance.','Can type checks, field-label rules, and source evidence prevent observed extraction errors from being reported as verified? This question focuses on the auditor’s ability to distinguish a plausible answer from a candidate linked to the correct kind of label.','How does strict NLI support checking compare with contradiction-only veto and rules-only verification on the project benchmark? This question evaluates whether requiring semantic entailment changes correctness or review volume relative to deterministic checks and contradiction detection.','What limitations remain before the prototype can be used with independent, real-world invoices? This question considers data representativeness, privacy, page provenance, OCR, security, operational resilience, and human approval.']: N(x)
P('The questions are answered using repository implementation evidence, unit-test coverage descriptions, benchmark scorecards, and a comparison of NLI modes. The benchmark is not large enough to support broad claims about the population of commercial invoices; the questions are therefore framed around the prototype and its declared data rather than universal system performance.')
H('1.6 Expected Outcomes',2); P('The expected engineering outcome is a runnable prototype with asynchronous PDF handling, auditable field results, compliance flags, bounded repair, a basic review queue, unit-test coverage, and a reproducible evaluation script. The academic outcome is a measured account of performance on a declared benchmark, including a discussion of the risks of synthetic data and tuning against observed errors.')
P('At the user level, the expected outcome is a document identifier returned after upload, a status that communicates processing progress, and a result view containing fields, compliance information, and audit details. At the system level, the expected outcome is a modular pipeline that separates ingestion, retrieval, extraction, compliance, verification, and persistence. At the evaluation level, the expected outcome is a scorecard that reports both correctness and coverage, including whether unsupported or absent fields were incorrectly accepted.')
P('The expected research outcome is not a claim that the project has solved general invoice understanding. It is a documented case study of how an extractive pipeline can be paired with deterministic checks, optional NLI, one constrained repair pass, and a review state. The report identifies what the small benchmark supports, which design choices were informed by the ablation, and which further evidence would be needed to make a stronger claim.')
P('The expected future-work outcome is a prioritized set of requirements for a more reliable system: independent data, richer source provenance, OCR and layout support, a reviewer correction workflow, secure upload and access controls, explicit model/version capture, and measured operational capacity. These outcomes provide a path from an academic prototype toward a controlled pilot without presenting that pilot as already implemented.')
H('1.7 Organization of the Report',2)
P('The report is organized from project motivation to implementation evidence and then to operational recommendations. Chapters 2 and 3 introduce the relevant concepts, requirements, and scope. Chapters 4 and 5 describe system architecture, data flow, implementation modules, and persistent records. Chapter 6 formalizes the field schema, verification rules, repair path, and evaluation metrics. Chapters 7 and 8 present the test strategy, benchmark results, and their interpretation, while Chapter 9 discusses limitations, privacy, and risk.')
P('Chapters 10 through 15 summarize conclusions, future work, use cases, operating guidance, deployment, test engineering, and project feasibility. Chapters 16 through 21 provide a deeper component walkthrough, data dictionary, security analysis, reproducibility guidance, design alternatives, and an extended implementation plan. The appendices contain API and setup references, repository structure, field rules, illustrative examples, operational procedures, annotation guidance, release-record templates, and a glossary. References are listed before the appendices.')
H('2. Background and Related Concepts')
H('2.1 Information Extraction',2); P('Question-answering models identify spans in context in response to natural-language questions. DocuAudit uses fixed questions for a seven-field invoice schema; it aims to return text already present in the PDF rather than free-form prose. The selected RoBERTa checkpoint is published as a SQuAD2 question-answering model [1].')
P('For each invoice, the extractor asks one primary question per schema field and searches for an answer span in the full context. The implementation truncates the model input to a maximum sequence length of 512 tokens and considers answer spans up to 30 tokens. The returned answer is reduced to its first line and may be extended to the end of that source line when the model appears to have stopped part-way through a value. This design favors short, source-grounded outputs but means relevant text beyond the model context window can be missed.')
P('Information extraction converts unstructured or semi-structured document content into named fields that a later application can store and compare. In invoice processing, a field schema provides the target structure, while the PDF provides the evidence from which values must be selected. Different implementation families solve this task in different ways: fixed templates and regular expressions depend on known layouts; OCR converts image pixels into text; named-entity or sequence models identify token categories; and question-answering systems select a text span in response to a field-specific question. These approaches have different assumptions about layout, field wording, and the quality of source text.')
P('DocuAudit uses extractive question answering because the initial scope is native-text invoices and the target schema is fixed. The model is asked a natural-language question such as “What is the invoice date?” and returns a span from the supplied context. Returning a source span makes it possible to compare the value with invoice text, but it does not guarantee that the span answers the intended question. A model may choose a nearby date or label, or return a value from a semantically similar field. That is why the answer is treated as a candidate rather than a verified result.')
P('The extraction module uses one primary wording per field on the first pass and holds alternate question phrasings for repair. This design limits model calls during the ordinary path while allowing the repair path to vary the question and context when the initial candidate fails audit. It is a bounded strategy rather than an exhaustive search: if the text layout is unusual or the relevant passage falls outside the model context, the pipeline may still send the field to review.')
H('2.2 Retrieval and Semantic Search',2); P('Sentence-transformer embeddings represent text as vectors for semantic retrieval. The project uses BAAI bge-small-en-v1.5 with Qdrant cosine similarity and a document-ID filter [2]. The current worker indexes the whole extracted invoice as one chunk, so retrieval is document-scoped but offers little fine-grained ranking within long documents.')
P('An embedding maps text into a numeric vector intended to place semantically related texts near one another in vector space. A vector database stores those representations and returns points whose vectors are similar to a query vector. In a document-processing system, retrieval can be used to select relevant pages or passages before asking a model to extract a field. Filtering results by document identifier is essential in a shared collection because a semantically similar passage from another invoice must not be used as evidence for the current document.')
P('The current pipeline asks a broad query about invoice details and filters Qdrant points to the active document. The worker indexes the joined text as one chunk, so for a short invoice retrieval usually returns the document text as a whole. This supports an integrated retrieval component but does not demonstrate fine-grained passage ranking. For longer documents, chunking by page or section could improve context selection; however, chunking also requires decisions about overlap, page metadata, label/value boundaries, and how evidence is mapped back to the original source.')
P('Vector search is complementary to, rather than a replacement for, explicit label rules. A vector similarity score measures a relation between text representations and a query; it does not prove that an amount is the total or that a date is the due date. A lexical search can be a useful baseline for invoices because fields often have explicit labels. A future retrieval experiment should compare lexical, vector, and hybrid methods using retrieval recall, field accuracy, review rate, and runtime rather than assuming one method is best for every document.')
H('2.3 Verification and NLI',2); P('The audit combines deterministic checks with a text-pair classifier. Rules reject empty or ill-typed values, missing source matches, and evidence carrying incompatible labels. An optional DeBERTa NLI model classifies a labelled line against a plain-language claim as entailment, contradiction, or neutral [3]. NLI is an additional check, not proof of truth; project evaluation found strict support requirements could increase unnecessary review.')
P('Verification asks a different question from extraction. Extraction proposes a value; verification tests whether that value is sufficiently supported by the source under the project’s rules. DocuAudit first checks whether the value has a plausible type, then searches for the value in extracted text, then checks whether the containing line has a compatible field label and no known conflicting label. This order catches inexpensive structural mistakes before invoking a language model. For example, a value can be a valid date yet still be rejected as a due date if it occurs only beside an invoice-date label.')
P('Natural-language inference provides a second kind of semantic comparison. The auditor forms a premise from labelled evidence and a hypothesis from a plain-language field claim. The NLI classifier assigns a label and score. In veto mode, a sufficiently confident contradiction can reject a candidate, while a neutral result does not override successful deterministic checks. In strict mode, the premise must entail the hypothesis above the threshold. In off mode, the model is not loaded and the deterministic rules decide. These modes encode different operating policies, not interchangeable confidence levels.')
P('A model score should not be interpreted as a calibrated probability that a value is true. NLI can be affected by wording, context length, model training data, and label semantics. Deterministic rules can also be wrong when labels are unfamiliar or text extraction has disrupted reading order. The project therefore combines evidence sources and reports the policy, while acknowledging that neither an NLI result nor a rule pass proves invoice authenticity or business correctness.')
P('The separation between candidate value, evidence, and support decision is a form of decision transparency. A user can see that a model proposed a value but the auditor did not accept it, or inspect the line that supported an accepted value. This is more informative than returning only a completed JSON object. The current implementation still lacks page-coordinate provenance and a structured reviewer correction record, so transparency is limited to extracted text and stored audit fields.')
H('2.4 Human Review',2); P('A review state is an explicit outcome. If a field remains unsupported after repair, the document is marked needs_review and shown in the interface queue. The current application does not let a reviewer edit, approve, or correct the document.')
P('Human-in-the-loop processing combines automated decisions with a person’s ability to resolve ambiguity or correct an output. In a cautious workflow, automation handles fields that meet defined evidence requirements, while uncertain cases are presented to a reviewer. This pattern can reduce the pressure to guess, but it shifts some workload to people and requires a clear interface that distinguishes verified information from suggestions.')
P('DocuAudit implements the routing part of this pattern but not the full resolution loop. The application lists needs_review documents and displays extracted fields and diagnostics. It does not provide assignment, field correction, approval, reviewer notes, or a durable history of human decisions. Accordingly, the report describes the interface as a basic review queue rather than a complete human-approval system. The distinction matters because a queue that can show uncertainty does not by itself establish that the uncertainty was resolved.')
P('The one-pass repair stage is an automated recovery attempt before human review. It tries alternative phrasings and values near field labels, then retains a candidate only if the same audit accepts it. This is a constrained form of self-correction: the model may propose alternatives, but it cannot override the evidence policy. Limiting repair to one round bounds processing and makes the terminal outcome easier to reason about; it can also leave recoverable values unsupported when the candidate-generation strategy is too narrow.')
H('2.5 Compliance Screening and Field Verification',2)
P('Field verification and compliance screening are related but distinct functions. Field verification asks whether an individual value is supported as the requested field. Compliance screening looks for selected document-level conditions such as arithmetic inconsistency, an amount above a configured threshold, a repeated invoice number, or detected person/location entities. One function cannot substitute for the other: a total may be clearly printed and pass field verification while still failing the arithmetic consistency rule.')
P('In DocuAudit, compliance results contain a list of flags, a simple risk-level value, and selected NER detections. The amount rule compares subtotal plus tax with total using a fixed tolerance; another rule compares total with a configured threshold; duplicate detection checks invoice numbers for a normalized vendor; and the NER component returns person and location entities. These are screening signals for inspection, not a complete regulatory compliance engine. The project does not establish currency-aware policy interpretation, supplier validation, tax-law analysis, or payment authorization.')
P('The distinction also affects evaluation. Field metrics compare extracted values and audit decisions with gold field labels, while compliance flag accuracy compares the predicted flag set with the expected set for each invoice. An invoice can have excellent field extraction and still receive an incorrect compliance flag. Reporting these results separately helps identify whether an observed weakness lies in value extraction, evidence validation, or document-level rule logic.')
H('2.6 Asynchronous Processing and Workflow State',2)
P('Asynchronous processing is a design pattern in which a client submits work and receives an identifier before the long-running task has completed. A background worker later performs the work, while the client checks status or receives a notification. This pattern avoids requiring the browser and API request to remain connected throughout model loading, inference, retrieval, and persistence.')
P('The project uses Celery with Redis to queue work. The API creates a document record and returns its ID; the worker changes the record through pending and processing states and finally writes complete, needs_review, or failed. The frontend polls the detail endpoint. This creates eventual consistency: a client can briefly observe a pending state after a task has been queued, and a browser timeout does not necessarily mean the worker failed. Durable state in PostgreSQL is therefore the source for later status queries, while Redis carries work messages.')
P('A status model should communicate both execution and decision outcomes. Failed means processing was interrupted by an exception; needs_review means processing finished but at least one field remained unsupported; complete means all submitted fields passed the configured audit. These states should not be collapsed into one success boolean. In larger deployments, queue retries, duplicate task delivery, idempotency, queue monitoring, and dependency readiness also become important because asynchronous systems can expose partial or delayed work.')
H('2.7 Data Provenance and Reproducible Evaluation',2)
P('Data provenance describes how a result can be traced to its source, transformation, and decision history. In invoice automation, useful provenance includes the source file, its hash, the page and location of evidence, the extracted text span, the model and rule version, and any human correction. DocuAudit currently retains source files and text-line evidence but does not preserve page coordinates, a cryptographic source reference in the audit record, or populated model-version metadata.')
P('Evaluation reproducibility requires more than a score. A result should be tied to a code revision, dependencies, model checkpoints, NLI mode and threshold, dataset version, annotation policy, and scoring implementation. The repository includes a golden dataset and score scripts, which provide a basis for repeated measurement. However, the ten examples first described as held out were later merged into the development set after their failures informed changes. The final development results must therefore not be described as an independent test result.')
P('This background motivates the report’s emphasis on raw counts, multiple metrics, and explicit limitations. Field F1 reflects similarity of extracted text to gold values; verified precision concerns the correctness of accepted values; coverage concerns how many present values are accepted; absent-field handling concerns hallucinated values; and flag accuracy concerns compliance rules. No single measure captures all of these behaviors, and results on synthetic data do not prove performance on real-world invoices.')
H('2.8 Relationship of Concepts to DocuAudit',2)
P('The concepts in this chapter are combined in a deliberate sequence. Text extraction converts a PDF into a form that the models can read. Retrieval supplies document-scoped context. Question answering proposes values for a fixed schema. Type, source, and label rules validate candidate structure and evidence. NLI optionally provides an additional semantic check. Compliance logic evaluates selected invoice-level conditions. Bounded repair searches for an alternative when a field is unsupported, and the asynchronous workflow persists a final state for a user to inspect.')
P('Each concept addresses a different failure mode, and each introduces its own limitations. Text extraction can lose layout; retrieval can select overly broad context; question answering can select the wrong span; rules can miss unfamiliar labels; NLI can be uncertain; compliance rules can oversimplify policy; and human review can remain incomplete if the interface has no correction action. The system’s overall trustworthiness therefore depends on the full chain and on the quality of its evaluation data, not on the presence of any single model or component.')
P('The project should be understood as an evidence-oriented prototype. It demonstrates a way to make uncertain extraction visible, measures selected behaviors on a controlled dataset, and identifies work needed for broader deployment. The next chapters translate these concepts into explicit requirements, system design, implementation detail, test evidence, measured results, and an extended plan for further development.')

H('3. Requirements and Scope')
H('3.1 Functional Requirements',2)
T(['ID','Requirement','Implementation'],[('FR-1','Accept PDF and return a document ID.','POST /documents saves file and queues Celery.'),('FR-2','Process asynchronously and expose status.','pending, processing, complete, needs_review, failed.'),('FR-3','Extract seven invoice fields.','Fixed schema and question templates.'),('FR-4','Audit candidates with field evidence.','Type/label rules, source lines, optional NLI.'),('FR-5','Detect compliance conditions.','Arithmetic, threshold, duplicates, NER.'),('FR-6','Repair once and route unresolved fields.','Candidate must pass audit.'),('FR-7','Present results and review queue.','Streamlit UI.')])
P('The requirements reflect a single-document workflow. An upload creates a record that can be polled by ID; the interface is not expected to hold a long-running HTTP request open while model inference runs. The audit requirement applies independently to each extracted field, so a partially supported invoice can be completed as a processing task while still receiving the needs_review outcome. This keeps pipeline completion distinct from acceptance of all extracted data.')
H('3.2 Non-Functional Requirements',2)
for x in ['Traceability: return per-field support decisions and evidence text.','Reliability: isolate long-running inference from the request path and record terminal status.','Maintainability: separate agents, workflow, API, persistence, and evaluation.','Reproducibility: Docker Compose deployment and pinned core packages.','Safety orientation: emphasize verified precision and route unresolved fields to review.']: B(x)
H('3.3 Scope and Assumptions',2); P('The implementation is limited to native-text invoice PDFs and a fixed schema. It does not include OCR for scans, robust table parsing, general document understanding, or an operational approval workflow. It assumes PyMuPDF can extract useful text and that field labels are sufficiently informative. The compliance amount threshold is 10,000; no currency or jurisdiction-specific policy interpretation is established.')
H('3.4 Constraints and Acceptance Criteria',2)
T(['Constraint','Design response','Acceptance evidence'],[('Long-running model inference','Queue work through Celery and Redis.','Upload returns an ID before processing completes.'),('Hallucinated or mismatched values','Separate extraction from evidence audit.','Wrong values should remain unsupported.'),('Variable invoice text','Use label alternatives and limited candidate repair.','Golden cases cover varied labels and stacked layouts.'),('Unavailable field','Permit an empty or unsupported result.','Absent due-date examples are routed to review.'),('Regression risk','Run a golden-set gate.','Gate fails when any baseline metric falls below threshold.')])
P('The repository does not specify a formal latency SLA, concurrency target, or production availability target. Accordingly, the project evaluates functional behavior and benchmark accuracy but does not claim a service-level objective.')

